Relate a closed boundary flux to a volume derivative (@math-fields-gauss: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Derive-the-surface-circulation-identity-from-a-parameter-patch.lingua#r_00FB68AFB25B0AA0ABA7407DAB") { r_00066E42C5FCE3F4B938BBA483
For F∈C¹ near the closed box V=[a,b]×[c,d]×[e,f], the x-face pair is ∬[P(b,y,z)−P(a,y,z)]dy dz. By the fundamental theorem and continuous iterated integration it equals ∭P_x dV. The y and z pairs give ∭Q_y and ∭R_z. Adding proves ∬_∂V F·n dA=∭_V divF dV. Adjacent boxes have exactly cancelling internal faces, so the proof extends to finite box unions with matching traces.

The general divergence theorem applies to bounded solids with suitably piecewise smooth closed boundary, outward orientation, and C¹ fields on a neighborhood of the entire solid closure. It is used here as an accepted theorem beyond the proved box cases. [Calculus and differential-equation preparation — Strang and Herman, OpenStax](../../chemistry/industrial-chemistry/sources/Calculus-and-Differential-Equations-OpenStax.lingua#r_027WKVM5NG5X84M86TSVRA77J3), Volume 3 section 6.8 Theorem 6.20 states these regularity requirements and labels its small-box discussion informal. Comparing area or surface lengths of staircase approximations is not a substitute for the missing convergence argument. More general rough domains and weak formulations belong to later analysis.

Take F=(x²+2y,3y²−z,2z²+4x) on [0,2]×[−1,1]×[0,3]. The divergence is 2x+6y+4z. Its volume integral is 96. The six outward face integrals, ordered low/high x, low/high y, low/high z, are 0,24,−9,9,−16,88. Their sum is independently 96. The cross terms affect individual faces while cancelling in opposing pairs; do not infer that a term absent from the divergence is absent from local boundary flow.

A domain check is indispensable. F=x/|x|³ is smooth away from the origin and has divergence 3r⁻³−3r²r⁻⁵=0 there. On a sphere of radius R centered at zero, its outward flux is (1/R²)(4πR²)=4π. Applying the theorem to the full ball would be invalid because F is undefined at zero. On a shell ε≤r≤R, the inner boundary points toward the origin and contributes −4π, cancelling the outer +4π. This uses the accepted general theorem consistently; a singular source or distributional extension requires further definitions.

**Work:** reconstruct the polynomial face values without using divF, then calculate the volume integral without using those face values. Change a coefficient and predict which faces and which derivative terms change. State the actual domain before applying any cancellation. Compare the already developed [Gradient, divergence, curl and integral theorems](../../physics/electromagnetism/mathematics/Gradient-divergence-curl-and-integral-theorems.lingua#r_05M47N16QDCC5B927TEYXAMMA4) singular example with this explicit account.

